00:00:27.450 --> 00:00:33.490
So, the subject of old age and
dementia, Alzheimer's uh, it,

00:00:33.495 --> 00:00:34.770
it comes up fairly frequently.

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:39.840
Uh, I think as people get older,
they're obviously, you know, concerned

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:44.700
that they might start showing
symptoms of Alzheimer's or dementia.

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:45.790
Uh, I think...

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:48.750
I'm 77 at times uh, I forget things.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:52.290
I'm not sure whether I'm more
forgetful now than I was before.

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:54.000
I was always a little forgetful.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.900
Uh, we tend to interpret any sort
of moments where we forget things

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:03.720
as an example of, uh, declining, you
know, brain function or whatever.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:09.030
And to some extent, probably as we get
older, just as our bodies are not as, as

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:14.695
strong, uh, perhaps our brains are also
slowly, uh, you know, not functioning

00:01:14.699 --> 00:01:19.634
as, as well as they did, uh, you know,
earlier on, but that's not the same as

00:01:19.664 --> 00:01:21.854
obviously as as dementia or Alzheimer's.

00:01:22.485 --> 00:01:25.725
So very often you hear that
learning another language can

00:01:25.725 --> 00:01:27.375
help stave off Alzheimer's.

00:01:27.794 --> 00:01:32.294
Not entirely clear whether this means
someone who already is bilingual or

00:01:32.294 --> 00:01:38.235
multilingual and therefore has these
defenses against the onset of Alzheimer or

00:01:38.445 --> 00:01:44.265
if someone takes up the study of languages
in old age or older, when they're older,

00:01:44.265 --> 00:01:46.634
does that help stave off dementia?

00:01:46.845 --> 00:01:50.554
And it's easy enough to Google and
look up, and you'll find a, a, a

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:52.215
variety of articles on this subject.

00:01:52.815 --> 00:01:56.745
Uh, the gist of it is that
learning languages is good for you.

00:01:57.255 --> 00:02:00.164
Any kind of brain
activity is good for you.

00:02:00.164 --> 00:02:04.425
Doing Sudoku, playing the piano,
anything that activates the

00:02:04.429 --> 00:02:05.895
brain cells is better for you.

00:02:06.885 --> 00:02:11.355
Sitting in front of the television,
for example, um, which is not an

00:02:11.355 --> 00:02:15.195
activity that engages the brain
a lot, reading actually does.

00:02:15.615 --> 00:02:20.325
So any activity like that, I, I would
imagine, and I'm sure there's lots

00:02:20.325 --> 00:02:24.285
of research on it, shows that you're
helping to delay the potential onset

00:02:24.315 --> 00:02:29.745
of dementia in terms of evaluating, you
know, being bilingual or multilingual

00:02:29.745 --> 00:02:34.660
or learning other languages and
the effect that has on dementia.

00:02:34.665 --> 00:02:39.120
There are studies, percentage
improvement or, uh, you know, delay

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:41.130
the onset by a few years or whatever.

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:45.630
However, these studies, I don't think
they've done a good job of sort of

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:49.350
isolating for example, you know,
people in different socioeconomic

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:54.000
circumstances, uh, how well do you
actually have to speak the language for

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.310
it to have a, an effect or benefit you?

00:02:57.015 --> 00:03:00.225
Is it enough that you were,
you know, bilingual as a kid?

00:03:00.255 --> 00:03:02.805
Do you have to be continuing
to learn these languages?

00:03:02.924 --> 00:03:05.595
There's so many unanswered
questions there, but I just

00:03:05.595 --> 00:03:06.734
take it that it's good for you.

00:03:07.245 --> 00:03:11.535
So learning languages is gonna
help you stave off dementia.

00:03:12.225 --> 00:03:16.215
Uh, but there's another aspect to that
and to this and that is, it's not just

00:03:16.215 --> 00:03:19.305
that you're learning languages and you're
activating certain parts of your brain.

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:23.310
I think it's the fact that if you
commit to learning a language at

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:27.570
my age, let's say, or even 10 years
younger or 10 years older, that

00:03:27.575 --> 00:03:29.040
means you have a purpose in life.

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:33.720
So there seems, there's quite a
bit of research to show that it's

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:36.090
not even enough to be positive.

00:03:36.095 --> 00:03:41.100
We all like to be positive, but actually
to feel you have a purpose and, uh, I feel

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:43.870
a sense of purpose in learning languages.

00:03:43.875 --> 00:03:44.700
I enjoy doing it.

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:51.390
It gives me satisfaction and I,you
know, acquiring knowledge is a, a, a,

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:53.760
a perfectly legitimate purpose in life.

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:59.160
I just happen to be reading a book about
the history of Arabs written by Albert

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:05.265
Hourani, and he talks about Ibn Khaldun
the famous, uh, Arab, uh, traveler and

00:04:05.265 --> 00:04:08.325
and writer in the maybe 14th century.

00:04:08.924 --> 00:04:12.674
And, uh, he spent his mornings
working for the visa for the

00:04:12.679 --> 00:04:16.334
government and in the afternoon he
studied, like that was his activity.

00:04:16.425 --> 00:04:19.755
Learning learning is a
perfectly legitimate activity.

00:04:19.755 --> 00:04:21.644
It's a constructive purpose.

00:04:22.335 --> 00:04:26.534
And so if we have that purpose and we
feel that purpose, then we are less likely

00:04:26.534 --> 00:04:28.575
to suffer from Alzheimer's it turns out.

00:04:28.815 --> 00:04:31.695
Whereas people who don't have a
sense of purpose when they're older,

00:04:31.875 --> 00:04:36.974
they're more likely to, you know,
have these kinds of, uh, difficulties.

00:04:37.724 --> 00:04:42.015
So it's not enough just to be positive
because the positive can go up and down.

00:04:42.284 --> 00:04:46.495
I was not that positive about my Arabic
because I wasn't making much progress.

00:04:47.015 --> 00:04:49.155
And then strange things happen.

00:04:49.365 --> 00:04:53.145
Amazon delivery, the
delivery man was from Yemen.

00:04:53.175 --> 00:04:57.405
We speak a few words in Arabic, I'm
re-motivated, so then when I work

00:04:57.405 --> 00:04:59.595
out on my treadmill, I go and grab...

00:04:59.595 --> 00:05:04.185
or if I, I go to Netflix and find
an an Egyptian movie to watch.

00:05:04.425 --> 00:05:07.515
And so all of a sudden I'm more
positive about my Arabic little things.

00:05:07.515 --> 00:05:10.125
You know, sometimes you're a little
bit down because you can't remember

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:13.635
stuff, and then something happens to
make you more positive, but beyond

00:05:13.635 --> 00:05:17.490
that having this sense that you have
a purpose, whatever that purpose may

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:24.000
be, uh, apparently is very powerful in
staving off the onset of Alzheimer's.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:29.970
So couple that with the fact that
learning languages has some not

00:05:30.450 --> 00:05:35.580
totally, clearly defined, beneficial
effect, uh, in providing you with, with

00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:37.500
better defense against Alzheimer's.

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:42.390
Uh, I think those are very good reasons
for learning languages well into old age.

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:44.325
So I leave you with those thoughts.

00:05:44.325 --> 00:05:48.015
I'd be interested in what you have
to say, and uh, it's just another

00:05:48.015 --> 00:05:50.475
reason why language shorting
is such a good thing to do.

00:05:50.895 --> 00:05:51.915
Thank you for listening.

00:05:51.945 --> 00:05:52.305
Bye for now.

